.TH yamlschema 5 "" YAMLSchema YAMLSchema
This page specifies the human-authored YAMLSchema DSL and its expansion to the
canonical \fB\&.ysdc\fR model, serialized as \fB\&.ysdc.yaml\fR or \fB\&.ysdc.json\fR\&.
A schema defines types: sets of constraints for a scalar, mapping, or list of
another type.

.PP
The optional top-level \fB\&.ysid\fR is a non-empty document identity string:

.EX
\&.ysid: https://example.com/contact.ysd.yaml
.EE

.PP
Generated output places \fB\&.ysid\fR first, although input may place it anywhere.
\&.ysd and .ysdc use \fB\&.ysd.yaml\fR, while JSON Schema uses \fB\&.schema.json\fR\&.
Conversion replaces a recognized suffix and appends the target suffix when
none is present.
JSON Schema \fB$id\fR and Draft 4 root \fBid\fR both import as \fB\&.ysid\fR\&.

.PP
The optional \fB\&.name\fR directive gives a schema node an externally addressable
name:

.EX
\&.name: PersonSchema

+address:
  .name: AddressSchema
  street: +Str
.EE

.PP
At the document top it names the root schema.
Inside a definition or property type it names that schema node.
It maps directly to JSON Schema \fB$anchor\fR, while a definition key such as
\fB+address\fR remains the local YAMLSchema type name.
Names must match \fB[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9._-]*\fR and must be unique in one schema
document.

.PP
The optional \fB\&.root\fR directive distinguishes an explicit root type from a
document that only publishes named definitions:

.EX
\&.root: {}

+kind: +Str
.EE

.PP
This example has a closed, empty mapping as its root type, so only \fB{}\fR is
valid at the document root.
Without \fB\&.root\fR, the document only defines \fB+kind\fR and leaves its JSON Schema
root unconstrained.
\fB\&.root\fR may also reference a named type, as in \fB\&.root: +kind\fR\&.

.SH Types in Mappings
A normal mapping entry has the data key on the left and its type on the right:

.EX
name: +Str
nickname?: +Str
.EE

.PP
Keys are required unless they end in \fB?\fR\&.
The \fB?\fR remains on the key in canonical YAMLSchema.
A key enclosed by \fB/\fR is a JSON Schema property-name pattern:

.EX
/^x-/: +Any
headers:
  '/^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9-]*$/': +Str
.EE

.PP
Pattern keys may match zero or more mapping keys, so they are never required.
The text between the first and last slash is the exact
\fBpatternProperties\fR key.
No anchors are added and no characters are unescaped.
For example, \fB//foo//\fR represents the JSON Schema pattern \fB/foo/\fR\&.
Pattern keys cannot use \fB\&.need\fR or appear in \fB\&.keys\fR rules.
They may coexist with ordinary properties and the \fB+Str\fR wildcard.
The slash-delimited form is reserved, so an exact property name such as
\fB/name/\fR cannot be represented as an ordinary mapping key.
A value may be a reference, an anonymous type, or a reference refined by more
constraints:

.EX
email: +email
code: +Str /[A-Z][0-9]+/
work_email: +email /@example\\.com$/
.EE

.PP
Definitions use a \fB+slug\fR key.
The key names the type and references use the same slug:

.EX
+resources:
  requests?: +Map{+Any}
  limits?: +Map{+Any}

resources?: +resources
.EE

.PP
Local references name definitions with \fB+name\fR\&.
External JSON Schema references use \fB+Ref(...)\fR and preserve the reference
text without fetching or resolving it:

.EX
author: +Ref(https://example.com/user-profile.schema.json)
profile?: +Ref(../schemas/profile.json#/$defs/profile)
.EE

.PP
The compact form requires a non-empty reference without whitespace or \fB)\fR\&.
It accepts the normal list and nullable suffixes, such as \fB+Ref(#item)[]\fR and
\fB+Ref(profile.json)~\fR\&.
Use the canonical \fB\&.xref\fR directive for every other reference string or when
the reference has sibling constraints:

.EX
author:
  .xref: https://example.com/user-profile.schema.json
  .desc: An externally defined author
empty:
  .xref: ''
.EE

.PP
\fB\&.xref\fR accepts any string and exports it unchanged as JSON Schema \fB$ref\fR\&.
It is separate from \fB\&.from\fR, which imports schemas or namespaces.

.PP
Anonymous mapping shapes need no \fB\&.type\fR marker.
Shaped mappings are closed by default; key-side \fB+Str\fR admits otherwise
unmatched string keys:

.EX
labels:
  fixed?: +Str
  +Str: +Str
.EE

.PP
String-keyed maps can name their value type directly:

.EX
config: +Map{+Any}
labels: +Map{+Str}
flags: +Map{+flag}
.EE

.PP
\fB+Map{+Type}\fR expands to \fB+Str: +Type\fR\&.
The value type must be one built-in, user-defined, or namespaced reference.
Map parameters use braces; parenthesized forms such as \fB+Map(+Any)\fR are
invalid.
An incomplete \fB+Map\fR must be completed by sibling key/value pairs.

.PP
Lists of maps append the list suffix to the value type:

.EX
maps: +Map{+Any}[]
other: +Map{+Str}[]
.EE

.PP
The reserved future form \fB+Map{+Key,+Value}\fR will support YAML key schemas.
It is not implemented yet.
The current one-reference form is shorthand for \fB+Map{+Str,+Value}\fR and
therefore fixes keys to strings.

.PP
Mappings are closed by default.
Top-level \fB\&.open: true\fR changes the default for the document mapping and every
mapping shape defined beneath it:

.EX
\&.open: true

+person:
  name: +Str

server:
  .open: false
  host: +Str
.EE

.PP
Here the document and \fB+person\fR are open, while \fBserver\fR and every mapping
shape nested beneath it are closed unless locally reopened.

.PP
A nested \fB\&.open\fR must be Boolean and overrides the inherited value.
An explicit \fB+Str\fR wildcard controls the current shape directly.
Combining \fB\&.open: false\fR with such a wildcard is an error.
Canonical .ysdc keeps \fB\&.open: true\fR only at the document top.
It uses \fB\&.open: false\fR to close a shape under an open default and a final
\fB+Str: +Any\fR wildcard to open a shape under a closed default.

.PP
The \fB\&.size\fR directive constrains the number of properties in a mapping.
It works on anonymous mapping shapes and at the document root:

.EX
\&.size: 1+
/^x-/: +Any

bounded:
  .size: 1-3
  /^item-/: +Str
.EE

.PP
These forms map to \fBminProperties: 1\fR, and to \fBminProperties: 1\fR plus
\fBmaxProperties: 3\fR, respectively.
Canonical .ysdc expands them to \fB\&.size: [1]\fR and \fB\&.size: [1, 3]\fR\&.

.SH Key/Value Pair Constraints
Use a top-level \fB\&.keys\fR sequence when a constraint relates multiple mapping
pairs:

.EX
token?: +Str
existingSecret?: +Str

\&.keys:
- .any:
  - token: +Str 8+
  - existingSecret: +Str 1+
.EE

.PP
Each \fB\&.any\fR branch is a partial mapping constraint.
The example requires at least one branch to match: \fBtoken\fR must be present and
have at least eight characters, or \fBexistingSecret\fR must be present and have
at least one character.

.PP
Plain branch keys are required.
A branch key ending in \fB?\fR is optional.
Unmentioned properties remain unaffected, and a branch does not create or
close an object type.

.PP
One \fB\&.keys\fR rule becomes a root JSON Schema \fBanyOf\fR\&.
Multiple rules all apply and become ordered members of a root \fBallOf\fR\&.
Each rule must currently contain exactly one \fB\&.any\fR entry with at least two
non-empty property-to-type mappings.

.SH Built-in Types
.TS
allbox;
l l
l l .
\fBType\fP	\fBAccepted value\fP
\fB+Any\fR	Any YAML value
\fB+Str\fR	A string
\fB+Int\fR	An integer
\fB+Float\fR	A YAML float-tagged value
\fB+Num\fR	Any numeric value: \fB+Int\fR or \fB+Float\fR
\fB+Bool\fR	\fBtrue\fR or \fBfalse\fR
\fB+Null\fR	\fBnull\fR
\fB+Map\fR	T{
A mapping shape completed by sibling property definitions
T}
\fB+Map{+Type}\fR	T{
A mapping with string keys and \fB+Type\fR values
T}
.TE

.PP
Capitalized type names are reserved for these built-ins and the \fB+One\fR,
\fB+All\fR, and \fB+Not\fR combinator heads.
A type reference beginning with a capital letter is rejected when it is not
one of those known names.
User-defined type references must not begin with a capital letter.

.PP
Examples:

.EX
anything: +Any
name: +Str
age: +Int
ratio: +Float
number: +Num
enabled: +Bool
nothing: +Null
metadata: +Map{+Any}
labels: +Map{+Str}
person:
  .type: +Map
  name: +Str
  age?: +Int
.EE

.PP
\fB+Float\fR follows YAML tagging rather than JSON Schema numeric semantics.
It accepts float-tagged values such as \fB1.0\fR, \fB\&.inf\fR, and \fB\&.nan\fR, but not the
integer-tagged value \fB1\fR\&.
Use \fB+Num\fR when both integer- and float-tagged values are valid.

.PP
JSON Schema has no float-only numeric type.
Exporting \fB+Float\fR therefore emits \fBtype: "number"\fR and a warning for that
loss of precision.

.PP
Draft 2020-12 string formats use qualified YAMLSchema types:

.EX
dateOfBirth: +JSON-Schema/date
createdAt: +JSON-Schema/date-time
contact?: +JSON-Schema/email
.EE

.PP
The complete supported set is:

.EX
date-time date time duration email idn-email hostname idn-hostname
ipv4 ipv6 uri uri-reference iri iri-reference uuid uri-template
json-pointer relative-json-pointer regex
.EE

.PP
Each \fB+JSON-Schema/name\fR type exports as JSON Schema \fBtype: string\fR with
\fBformat: name\fR\&.
Normal nullable and list suffixes apply, for example
\fB+JSON-Schema/date-time~\fR and \fB+JSON-Schema/email[]\fR\&.
Unknown qualified format names are rejected.

.PP
Bare \fB+Map\fR is intentionally incomplete.
It must have sibling property definitions, as in \fBperson\fR, and its \fB\&.type\fR
marker disappears during canonical expansion because the mapping shape already
implies the type.
Use \fB+Map{+Any}\fR for an otherwise unconstrained string-keyed mapping, or
\fB+Map{+Type}\fR to constrain every value.
The future two-argument form \fB+Map{+Key,+Value}\fR is reserved for mappings
whose keys also have a schema.

.PP
Built-ins can be modified by the rest of the DSL.
\fB+Type[]\fR is a list of that type, \fB+Type~\fR also accepts null, and \fB+Any(...)\fR,
\fB+One(...)\fR, \fB+All(...)\fR, and \fB+Not(...)\fR combine referenced types.
These are type expressions built from the built-ins, not additional built-in
scalar types.

.SH Tight Type Expressions
A tight type expression is a YAML plain scalar.
A type reference is normally first, but labeled clauses may appear in any
order:

.EX
+Base [(alternatives)] [list-suffix] [~] [pattern-or-range]
      [enum] [size] [constant] [default] [title] [description]
.EE

.PP
The core is normally a \fB+Type\fR reference, which later clauses refine:

.EX
foo: +Str /a.*b/
port: +Int 1..65535
mode: +Str [debug, info, error]
.EE

.PP
A bare regex or numeric range can still infer a built-in type.
A fractional range infers \fB+Num\fR because its interval may include integers.
Generated .ysd includes the inferred reference explicitly.

.PP
These expand to explicit directives.
Refined types are always materialized:

.EX
foo:
  .type: +Str
  .like: a.*b
port:
  .type: +Int
  .range: [1, 65535]
mode:
  .type: +Str
  .enum: [debug, info, error]
.EE

.PP
\fB=~"pattern"\fR matches the complete string; leading \fB^\fR and trailing \fB$\fR
anchors are implied.
\fBmatch:"pattern"\fR is an accepted alias.
\fBfind:"pattern"\fR searches within the string.
The \fB/pattern/\fR form is shorthand for \fBfind:"pattern"\fR and is available only
when the pattern contains neither whitespace nor \fB/\fR\&.
The quoted bodies cannot contain \fB"\fR\&.
In .ysd, use explicit \fB\&.match\fR or \fB\&.find\fR when no tight form can represent the
pattern.
Both imply \fB+Str\fR\&.
Generated .ysd uses the canonical \fB=~"pattern"\fR spelling.

.PP
Canonical .ysdc stores both forms as \fB\&.like\fR, containing the exact JSON Schema
pattern.
A match is bookended with \fB^\fR and \fB$\fR; a find is stored unchanged:

.EX
whole:
  .type: +Str
  .like: ^pattern$
search:
  .type: +Str
  .like: pattern
.EE

.PP
\fB\&.like\fR is accepted only in \fB\&.ysdc.yaml\fR and \fB\&.ysdc.json\fR\&.
Conversely, \fB\&.match\fR and \fB\&.find\fR are .ysd directives and are rejected in .ysdc
input.

.PP
Compact enums require an explicit type reference and comma-separated members:

.EX
mode: +Str [debug, info, error]
level: +Int [1, 2, 3]
logLevel: +Str [debug, =info, warning, error, fatal]
.EE

.PP
Members may contain letters, digits, whitespace, \fB\&.\fR, \fB-\fR, \fB_\fR, and \fB+\fR\&.
Whitespace around each comma-separated member is trimmed; whitespace inside a
member is preserved.
Thus \fB[foo,bar,foo bar]\fR and \fB[ foo, bar, foo bar ]\fR have the same meaning.
Quotes are not supported inside compact enums; use explicit \fB\&.enum\fR with a
YAML sequence for quoted or other punctuated values.
The base controls scalar parsing, so \fB+Str [true,1]\fR contains two strings.
Prefix one member with \fB=\fR to also set \fB\&.init\fR to that value.
At most one member may be marked.
Generated .ysd uses one space after every compact-enum comma.

.PP
\fB+Str ==User\fR becomes \fB\&.const: User\fR, the exact-value constraint corresponding
to JSON Schema \fBconst\fR\&.
\fB+Str =="foo bar"\fR is the quoted form, and \fBconst:User\fR is the labeled
alternative.
A bare literal remains an accepted inferred-type shorthand.
It also means a constant, while \fB=value\fR means only a default.
The \fB+\fR prefix is reserved for type expressions, so an unrecognized
plus-prefixed value is an error instead of an inferred string constant.

.SH Lists and Sizes
List suffixes are part of the value-side type expression:

.EX
names?: +Str[1+]
.EE

.PP
This expands to \fB\&.type: +Str[]\fR and \fB\&.size: [1]\fR\&.
The \fB[]\fR is part of the list type; \fB\&.size\fR carries its bounds.
Key-side list suffixes are rejected.

.TS
allbox;
l l
l l .
\fBSuffix\fP	\fBMeaning\fP
\fB[]\fR	List with no size constraint
\fB[n]\fR	Exactly \fBn\fR items
\fB[n-m]\fR	Between \fBn\fR and \fBm\fR items
\fB[n+]\fR	At least \fBn\fR items
\fB[!]\fR	Unique items
\fB[$]\fR	Scalar or list
\fB[n-m,$!]\fR	T{
Scalar or a unique list with the given size
T}
.TE

.PP
The scalar-or-list form is the compact spelling of a JSON Schema \fBanyOf\fR
whose two branches are the same item schema and a list of that item schema:

.EX
python?: +Str[$]
.EE

.EX
{
  "anyOf": [
    {"type": "string"},
    {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}
  ]
}
.EE

.PP
The converter recognizes either branch order and matching item constraints,
references, and JSON Schema format types.
An \fBanyOf\fR with extra branches or different scalar and item schemas stays
explicit.

.PP
A list of shaped mappings uses the incomplete \fB+Map\fR base and defines the item
properties alongside it:

.EX
extraEnv?:
  .type: +Map[1-10,$!]
  name: +Str
  value?: +Str
.EE

.PP
Here the value is either one mapping or a unique list of one through ten
mappings.
Each mapping has the sibling \fBname\fR and \fBvalue\fR properties.

.PP
List properties may be comma-separated, whitespace-separated, or adjacent.
For example, \fB[1-10,$!]\fR, \fB[1-10,$,!]\fR, \fB[1-10 $ !]\fR, and \fB[1-10$!]\fR are
equivalent.
Generated DSL uses the canonical \fB[size,$!]\fR order.
\fB[+]\fR is an input alias for \fB[1+]\fR\&.
The former \fB|\fR separator is rejected.
Multiple sizes or repeated \fB$\fR and \fB!\fR flags are errors.

.PP
A size clause also works after string, list, or mapping constraints:

.EX
code: +Str 8
names: +Str[] 1+
labels: +Map{+Any} 1-20
.EE

.PP
Canonical sizes contain one number for an open upper bound and two for a
bounded or exact size:

.EX
1+    -> [1]
10    -> [10, 10]
10-20 -> [10, 20]
.EE

.PP
Canonical ranges use the same structural convention, with \fBnull\fR for a missing
lower bound:

.EX
0..          -> [0]
1..10        -> [1, 10]
\&..-1         -> [null, -1]
0...         -> [0] plus .xmin: true
\&...10        -> [null, 10] plus .xmax: true
0..10 :xmin  -> [0, 10] plus .xmin: true
0..10 :xmax  -> [0, 10] plus .xmax: true
.EE

.PP
Both modifiers may follow one bounded range.
The explicit directives must be \fBtrue\fR and require their corresponding range
bounds.
The spelling \fB0...10\fR is ambiguous and is rejected.

.PP
The old \fB"*"\fR bound is invalid.

.SH Nulls and Annotations
Nullability is a value-side suffix:

.EX
enabled?: +Bool~
.EE

.PP
Tight annotations follow all constraints:

.EX
enabled?: +Bool~ =false title:"Enabled" "Enable the service"
label?: +Str ="pretty good"
mode?: type:+Str enum:[debug,info] init:info desc:"Log level"
.EE

.IP \(bu 2
\fB=value\fR is a single YAML scalar default.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB="..."\fR is a string default that may contain spaces.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtitle:"..."\fR is \fB\&.title\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
A final \fB"..."\fR is \fB\&.desc\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Labeled scalar clauses may occur in any order.
They are \fBtype\fR, \fBmatch\fR, \fBfind\fR, \fBconst\fR, \fBrange\fR, \fBsize\fR, \fBitem\fR, \fBsolo\fR,
\fBuniq\fR, \fBnull\fR, \fBinit\fR, \fBtitle\fR, \fBdesc\fR, and scalar \fBalso\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBenum:[...]\fR is the compact enum form.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB:need(name1,name2)\fR lists sibling properties required when this property
is present.
Structural \fB\&.one\fR, \fB\&.any\fR, \fB\&.all\fR, \fB\&.not\fR, \fB\&.with\fR, and \fB\&.when\fR values
remain explicit.

.PP
Two exact triplets protect text that YAML forbids in a plain scalar: \fB:\\\fR
represents colon-space, and \fB\\#\fR represents space-hash.
No other backslash sequence is special, so \fBfoo\\ bar\fR, \fB\\n\fR, and \fB\\t\fR remain
literal.
A body cannot contain a double quote.
Use the explicit directive when that is needed.
A scalar consisting entirely of a YAML-quoted string is a literal value, not
an annotation, because YAML does not preserve its original quote style.
The obsolete trailing single-quoted description form is an error.

.SH Hybrid Explicit Types
When one constraint is clearer explicitly, \fB\&.type\fR may contain the complete
tight expression and sibling directives add the exceptional parts:

.EX
foo:
  .type: +Str[] /a.*b/ 10-20
  .title: The "Good" Parts
.EE

.PP
This is equivalent to:

.EX
foo:
  .type: +Str[]
  .like: a.*b
  .size: [10, 20]
  .title: The "Good" Parts
.EE

.PP
Directive order is insignificant.
Expansion normalizes \fB\&.size\fR and emits a stable directive order.
A directive supplied by both the \fB\&.type\fR expression and a sibling is an error,
even when the values agree.

.SH Combinators
Reference-only alternatives have compact forms:

.EX
one: +One(+Str,+Int)
any: +Any(+foo,+bar)
all: +All(+foo,+bar)
neither: +Not(+foo,+bar)
values: +Any(+foo,+bar)[]
.EE

.PP
\fBOne\fR, \fBAny\fR, and \fBAll\fR require at least two references.
\fBNot\fR requires at least one; multiple references mean the value must match
none of them.
A list suffix follows the complete combinator type.

.PP
Multiple references without parentheses are an implicit conjunction.
The first is the base and the rest are additional constraint groups:

.EX
value: +base +constraint +other
.EE

.PP
Branches containing complete type definitions use explicit directives:

.EX
value:
  .one:
  - +Str 1+
  - name: +Str
.EE

.PP
At the document root, \fB\&.one\fR constrains the root value in addition to its
declared properties:

.EX
deviceType: +Str
\&.one:
- .xref: https://example.com/smartphone.schema.json
  deviceType?: +Str ==smartphone
- .xref: https://example.com/laptop.schema.json
  deviceType?: +Str ==laptop
.EE

.PP
Root branches are partial constraints, not standalone closed object types.
An optional property constrains that property when present without requiring
it again inside the branch.
Directives such as \fB\&.xref\fR apply alongside the branch properties.

.PP
A JSON Schema \fBoneOf\fR with exactly one required-only branch is equivalent to
that ordinary \fBrequired\fR constraint.
The importer therefore marks those property keys as required instead of
emitting a redundant \fB\&.one\fR block.
Other \fBoneOf\fR branches remain explicit, including branches with annotations
or additional constraints.

.SH Canonical Expansion
Compile human-authored YAML to canonical JSON with:

.EX
ysd -t ysdc -J contact.ysd.yaml
ysd -t ysdc -J -C contact.ysd.yaml
ysd -t ysdc -J values.schema.json
.EE

.PP
Omit \fB-J\fR or use \fB-Y\fR for canonical YAML.
Use \fB-f/--from ysd\fR, \fBysdc\fR, or \fBjsc\fR when a filename or stdin does not make
the source format clear.
File suffixes \fB\&.ysd.yaml\fR, \fB\&.ysd.json\fR, \fB\&.ysdc.yaml\fR, \fB\&.ysdc.json\fR,
\fB\&.schema.json\fR, \fB\&.schema.json.yaml\fR, \fB\&.schema.yaml\fR, and \fB\&.schema.yml\fR are
inferred automatically.

.PP
Canonical directives are emitted in this order:

.EX
\&.name .type .xref .open .need .item .one .any .all .not .like
\&.enum .const .range .size .solo .uniq .null .init .title
\&.desc .also .with .when
.EE

.PP
An unrefined built-in or named reference is emitted directly as a \fB+Type\fR
scalar.
This is the compact form of a mapping whose only pair would be \fB\&.type: +Type\fR\&.
An external reference may similarly use \fB+Ref(...)\fR; its canonical form is
\fB\&.xref\fR rather than \fB\&.type\fR\&.
When annotations, validation constraints, or shape entries share the mapping,
the reference or complete tight expression remains under \fB\&.type\fR\&.

.PP
List types append \fB[]\fR to the reference.
An unconstrained list is therefore emitted as a scalar such as \fB+Any[]\fR;
constrained lists use forms such as \fB\&.type: +Str[]\fR with \fB\&.size\fR or \fB\&.uniq\fR\&.
\fB\&.list\fR is currently rejected and reserved for a possible future
list-constraint model; it does not mean "convert this type into a list."

.PP
Unknown directives are errors.
\fB\&.need\fR is valid only in a property definition and contains a sequence of
sibling property names.
For example, \fB\&.need: [password]\fR on \fBuser\fR means that the presence of \fBuser\fR
requires \fBpassword\fR\&.
\fB\&.also\fR, \fB\&.with\fR, and \fB\&.when\fR may be retained in explicit YAMLSchema, but an
export that cannot represent one fails rather than silently discarding it.
\fB\&.pick\fR and the former \fB\&.oneof\fR, \fB\&.anyof\fR, and \fB\&.allof\fR names are rejected
with guidance to use \fB\&.one\fR, \fB\&.any\fR, and \fB\&.all\fR\&.

.PP
The former names \fB\&.titl\fR, \fB\&.just\fR, \fB\&.only\fR, \fB\&.mini\fR, and \fB\&.maxi\fR are rejected
